The recent discussion regarding the ability to save as a checkpoint to
come back to if you make a mistake, as provided by many games but
explicitly omitted by games in the roguelike genre, did raise one
interesting point: being able to save/restore at will can allow a
player to explore alternative solutions to a specific problem and thus
improve their overall gameplay.

However, a NetHack where you could save/restore like this would not be
NetHack at all, as much of the challenge of the game is that it is
unforgiving of mistakes such that you need to learn how to avoid
making them in the first place.

As save/restore allows a player to further explore the depth of the
game and learn more about it, it seems it would be a natural fit for
non-scoring explore mode.  Like with the existing explore mode, it it
clear that the game doesn't "really" count, scores would not be added
to the record, bones would not be left, and the like.  It becomes
another tool for exploring the depth of the game by removing the risk
of death so you can mess around and see things which will help you get
better at playing the real game when you start a new game in normal
mode.

As this feature allows a different sort of exploration than proof from
dying does (it lets you play with items in odd ways to see what they
do without permanently messing up the game, for instance), it's not
redundant with the existing features.  Rather, it should improve
explore mode, making it better at its stated purpose.  Note that it
still requires that you try things to see what they do, rather than,
say, asking around on #nethack.

Putting it into the core game in this way should also reduce incentive
for people to add it on their own to help them learn.


